  "full_response": "### The Comprehensive History of Artificial Intelligence (1950s - Present Day)\n\n#### I. Introduction\n\nArtificial Intelligence (AI) is a multifaceted field of study focusing on creating machines capable of performing tasks that typically require human intelligence. From early theoretical frameworks to modern-day autonomous systems, AI has undergone transformative growth over the decades. This guide traces the of AI, exploring significant milestones, breakthroughs, and major contributions from pioneering researchers.\n\n#### II. The Dawn of Artificial Intelligence: The 1950s\n\n##### 2.1 Early Beginnings\n\nThe term \"artificial intelligence\" was coined in 1956 at the Dartmouth, organized by John McCarthy, Marvin Minsky, Nathaniel Rochester, and Claude Shannon. The conference marked the birth of AI as a distinct academic discipline. McCarthy's groundbreaking paper discussed the potential of machines that think, not as a fanciful thought experiment, but as a science.\n\n##### 2.2 The Logic Theorist\n\nIn 1955, Allen Newell and Herbert A. Simon developed the Logic Theorist (LT), the first computer program capable of simulating a problem-solver. LT demonstrated that a human-like solution to problems could be achieved by breaking them into a series of logical steps.\n\n##### 2. Shakey the Robot\n\nRaymond L. Smith designed and built Shakey, the first autonomous robot capable of navigating and executing tasks on its own. Shakey combined perception, decision-making, and world modeling in the mid-1960s, effectively serving as a prototype for future AI research.\n\n#### III. Thement and Early Developments (1960s-1970s)\n\n##### 3.1 Asimov's Laws and Early AI Applications\n\nDuring the 1960s, Isaac Asimov published series of science fiction stories featuring robots that obeyed a set of Three Laws of Robotics, emphasizing the interplay between AI and human ethics. Meanwhile, researchers like Marvin Minsky constructed the first neural network, an early precursor to modern AI.\n\n##### 3.2 Machine Learning: Perceptron\n\nFrank Rosenblatt introduced the Perceptron, the first neural network model, during a tutorial in 1958. Though initially limited, these networks were instrumental in motivating the development of more sophisticated learning algorithms.\n\n##### 3.3 Expert Systems and Knowledge-Based AI\n\nThe 1970s saw the rise of expert systems, which applied AI to emulate the decision-making ability of human experts. Early examples include DENDRAL for chemical",
